{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XYtbKzUPfkeTa8NmcTas9M","type":"tool_result","content":"60\tan unquoted delimiter does not. Command substitutions inside double quotes run\n61\tregardless of the outer consumer. Same-command function and alias bodies are\n62\tvisible source; definitions from earlier calls or profiles are not.\n63\t\n64\tNormalize only known launchers and their option arity. Inspect brace expansions\n65\tby membership, including the rest of argv. Treat unknown launcher flags as\n66\tambiguous rather than silently guessing which token is the executable.\n67\tApply explicit depth, body-size, word-count, and expansion ceilings.\n68\t\n69\tMalformed recognized shell bodies fail closed even without a recognizable\n70\tdestructive signature. Infrastructure failures remain fail open with the\n71\texisting hook telemetry policy. Neither result proves that execution is safe.\n72\t\n73\t## Decisions and observability\n74\t\n75\tAn indirect destructive veto carries a class-specific `reason_code` and uses\n76\tthe existing `veto_triggered` action once per command. Do not register an\n77\tunconsumed action or emit raw command material through a new event shape.\n78\tMap each class to the existing forensic technique before acceptance.\n79\t\n80\tThe existing trio remains `destructive=True`, including its equivalent indirect\n81\tforms, so the fact-gate/citation contract is preserved. A computed canonical\n82\twrite remains `destructive=False`: a destructive-command citation cannot grant\n83\tpermission to edit a canonical file. The A-9 destructive tag is an unresolved\n84\tOwner decision (C13 P-4); this proposal does not decide it by analogy.\n85\t\n86\tUse two independent trusted startup controls, both enabled by default:\n87\t\n88\t| Switch explicitly set to `0` | Scope disabled |\n89\t| --- | --- |\n90\t| `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS` | Computed-verb and opaque-feeder heuristics |\n91\t| `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD` | The additional indirect-execution scan |\n92\t\n93\tNeither switch disables pre-existing checks or changes an earlier BLOCK.\n94\tRead them from `trusted_env`, never from mutable tool input. Unset controls\n95\tproduce no disabled event. Each explicit disable emits at most one\n96\t`learning_rail_disabled` record per session and switch, using atomic marker\n97\tcreation under the audit directory. Marker/storage failure must not crash the\n98\thook; a crash after marker creation can lose that telemetry record.\n99\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"00ec2b1d-64bd-4aca-88dc-c9646f7b6927","uuid":"94e1b8b1-a89e-438d-ae1a-35af451ad0d5","timestamp":"2026-11-13T16:43:07.877Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.nUJTgA/tree/.claude/adr/ADR-201-bash-guard-indirect-execution.md","content":"an unquoted delimiter does not. Command substitutions inside double quotes run\nregardless of the outer consumer. Same-command function and alias bodies are\nvisible source; definitions from earlier calls or profiles are not.\n\nNormalize only known launchers and their option arity. Inspect brace expansions\nby membership, including the rest of argv. Treat unknown launcher flags as\nambiguous rather than silently guessing which token is the executable.\nApply explicit depth, body-size, word-count, and expansion ceilings.\n\nMalformed recognized shell bodies fail closed even without a recognizable\ndestructive signature. Infrastructure failures remain fail open with the\nexisting hook telemetry policy. Neither result proves that execution is safe.\n\n## Decisions and observability\n\nAn indirect destructive veto carries a class-specific `reason_code` and uses\nthe existing `veto_triggered` action once per command. Do not register an\nunconsumed action or emit raw command material through a new event shape.\nMap each class to the existing forensic technique before acceptance.\n\nThe existing trio remains `destructive=True`, including its equivalent indirect\nforms, so the fact-gate/citation contract is preserved. A computed canonical\nwrite remains `destructive=False`: a destructive-command citation cannot grant\npermission to edit a canonical file. The A-9 destructive tag is an unresolved\nOwner decision (C13 P-4); this proposal does not decide it by analogy.\n\nUse two independent trusted startup controls, both enabled by default:\n\n| Switch explicitly set to `0` | Scope disabled |\n| --- | --- |\n| `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_HEURISTICS` | Computed-verb and opaque-feeder heuristics |\n| `CEO_BASH_INDIRECT_GUARD` | The additional indirect-execution scan |\n\nNeither switch disables pre-existing checks or changes an earlier BLOCK.\nRead them from `trusted_env`, never from mutable tool input. Unset controls\nproduce no disabled event. Each explicit disable emits at most one\n`learning_rail_disabled` record per session and switch, using atomic marker\ncreation under the audit directory. Marker/storage failure must not crash the\nhook; a crash after marker creation can lose that telemetry record.\n","numLines":40,"startLine":60,"totalLines":167}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XYtbKzUPfkeTa8NmcTas9M","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
