{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch1GJD7YpeWNSv7YVV3hQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014GXxNSssuNn1fUzs23TcYn","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"report":"SCOPE OF THIS CUT: payload 5/32 adds new files and nothing else. They are 8 fragments in .claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/ (normalization, posture_parser, posture_scan, posture_tables, rewrite_citation; fact_state, fact_canonical and decisions_cli were read for context), 6 in _lib/_budget_summary_parts/, and _lib/_ceo_boot_parts/__init__.py. I read them against the candidate facades (check_bash_safety.py, budget-summary.py, ceo-boot.py), check_canonical_edit.py with _lib/canonical_edit/*, bash_indirect_guard.py, check_arbitration_kernel.py, scripts/install.sh, _python-hook.sh and ADR-201. For the moved code I compared against the pre-split monolith kept in .claude/plans/PLAN-182/staged-w1/tree/.claude/hooks/check_bash_safety.py.\n\nWHAT I CHECKED, AND WHAT HOLDS\n\nMoved code: check_bash_safety.py\n- Load order and names:\n  - The facade execs the 8 fragments in a fixed order into its own globals.\n  - Every module-level dependency across fragments is defined earlier in that order. For example, posture_tables needs _e3_basename from fact_canonical, rewrite_citation needs _FORCE_PUSH_REWRITE_DISABLE_VAR from the end of normalization, and re, shlex and hashlib come from normalization.\n  - __file__ inside each fragment is the facade's, so _HOOKS_DIR is still .claude/hooks.\n  - Monkeypatch points and global lookup are unchanged, because the fragments run in one namespace.\n  - The monolith already had `from __future__ import annotations`, so the dataclass behaviour of Decision and Rewrite is unchanged.\n- Rules: decide_command matches the pre-split version check for check (credential leak, then canonical-path write, then posture toggle, env-guard, git-bypass, and the rm/reset/push trio with rawscan and the rewrite pilot). The PLAN-195 scan is appended only on the path that would otherwise allow, so it can add blocks and never removes one.\n- Constants and sensitive branches I spot-checked are byte-identical to the monolith:\n  - _SUBCOMMAND_SPLIT_RE, _CITE_READ_CAP_BYTES, _E4_GLOB_MIN_LITERALS and _E4_BRACE_MAX_WORDS;\n  - the rejection of plan-file citations;\n  - the expansion-pass suppression of the unparseable branch;\n  - the fail-open of _e3 on dependency failure.\n\nFail direction\n- Infrastructure faults fail open:\n  - A missing fragment raises at import with no decision on stdout. _python-hook.sh then passes the interpreter's rc 1 through without mapping it to exit 2, so the call is not blocked.\n  - A missing bash_indirect_guard, or an exception inside it, reaches main()'s catch-all, which allows.\n  - If _trusted_env is None, get_trusted raises AttributeError, which is caught, and the scan stays ON.\n- Matcher input fails closed:\n  - _e3 already blocks on a top-level shlex failure before the scan runs.\n  - The scanner blocks on unparseable recognised shell bodies and when it hits its limits.\n\nDeclared conditions as they apply here\n- Condition 6 holds: both switches come from the trusted_env startup snapshot, a value of exactly \"0\" disables, and heuristics= is passed through.\n- Condition 5 holds for this slice: _KERNEL_PATHS contains `.claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/**/*` (check_arbitration_kernel.py:314) plus bash_indirect_guard.py and bash_indirect_telemetry.py (:335-336).\n- Condition 7 holds: the facade is 42 lines, and the guard `.claude/hooks/_lib/**/*.py` (canonical_edit/guards.py:34) sentinel-gates all three parts directories. Logic moved out of the non-canonical budget-summary.py and ceo-boot.py therefore becomes stricter to edit, which is the declared re-patch burden.\n\nCross-module dependency\n- The _e3 interceptor's lazy `from check_canonical_edit import _CANONICAL_GUARDS, _fnmatch_segments` would fail open on ImportError. It still resolves: guards.__all__ re-exports _CANONICAL_GUARDS and canonical_edit.bind exports _fnmatch_segments from paths.__all__. The canonical-path write interceptor is not silently disarmed.\n\nInstall and upgrade\n- install.sh install_lib_selective copies each top-level _lib entry wholesale, excluding only tests, test_isolation.py, testing.py and __pycache__. All three parts directories ship.\n- _budget_summary_parts has no __init__.py, so it imports as a namespace package. That works on Python 3.9.\n\nbudget-summary.py\n- The facade rebinds every part function to its own globals. __file__-relative lookups (ceo-cost-transcripts.py, optimizer/model_normalize.py) therefore still resolve next to the script.\n- The facade's own _load_normalize_model_name overrides the part's copy.\n- SOURCE_CHOICES, AUDIT_BANNER and ROOT_ENV are recomputed after binding, and build_parser reads them only at call time.\n- No registered hook invokes budget-summary.py.\n\nceo-boot.py\n- _PARTS lists 10 names, which matches the 10 files on disk.\n- ceo-boot.py is a slash-command script, not a hook.\n\nPython and writes\n- Standard library only. No syntax newer than 3.9 (no `match`, no `zip(strict=)`, no runtime `X | None`). Local annotations are never evaluated.\n- No new write target appears in this slice.\n\nP0: none.\nFalse declared condition: none found in this slice.\n\nNEW FINDINGS (annex)\nNone at P1.\n\nP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n1. P2 — .claude/hooks/_lib/_bash_safety_parts/posture_scan.py, decide_command, `_is_destructive = indirect_hit.reason_code != \"bash_computed_canonical_target\"`.\n   - Problem: every indirect class except the canonical target gets destructive=True. That includes bash_indirect_limit, bash_indirect_unparseable_shell, bash_indirect_ambiguous_launcher, bash_indirect_computed_verb and bash_indirect_opaque_stdin.\n   - ADR-201:80-84 tags only the trio and \"its equivalent indirect forms\", and says \"The A-9 destructive tag is an unresolved Owner decision ... this proposal does not decide it by analogy\". The code decides it.\n   - Scenario: an adopter arms CEO_FACT_GATE_ENFORCE=1 in settings, or the CEO_DESTRUCTIVE_CITATION_GATE pilot. A fail-closed parse or limit denial, or a `... | bash` denial, can then be released by a verbatim transcript citation on an exact retry. The release path is audited, not silent.\n   - With the default settings the verdict is unchanged. The only effect is that the shadow fact gate now also writes state and telemetry for these denials.\n   - Minimal fix: map only the trio-equivalent codes to destructive=True, i.e. those returned via `_hit(code)` from _destructive(). Keep the limit, unparseable, ambiguous and heuristic codes at destructive=False until the Owner decides A-9.\n2. P2 — fact_canonical.py:146-153, with the citation gate in fact_state.py.\n   - Problem: when the fact gate in enforce mode reshapes a block, it builds a new Decision without reason_code. decisions_cli.py:195 then skips the veto_triggered emit that carries the bash_indirect_* class.\n   - Effect: forensic detail only, and only under opt-in enforce.\n   - Fix: pass reason_code=decision.reason_code into the rebuilt Decision.\n3. P2 — posture_scan.py decide_command, and decisions_cli.py:171.\n   - Problem: the switches are compared with `== \"0\"` without strip(). Sibling switches strip (_rawscan_enabled, for example), so a value like \" 0\" leaves the scan armed.\n   - This fails safe. Fix: `str(raw).strip() == \"0\"`, for consistency.\n4. P2 — normalization.py:4-56.\n   - Problem: the library fragment carries the monolith's shebang and full module docstring as a dead string after the real docstring. That text describes \"Registered in settings.json\" as if this file were the hook, and its numbered list skips step 3.\n   - Fix: trim it to a fragment docstring, or point it at the facade.\n5. P2 — budget-summary.py and _lib/_budget_summary_parts/.\n   - Dead code: cost_rollup.py's _load_normalize_model_name is overridden by the facade's copy, and the first 